@page "/projects/roomloom"
@layout ParkerPortfolio.Layouts.ProjectLayout

<PageTitle>RoomLoom · Aaron Joel Parker</PageTitle>

<div class="canvas">
    <a class="back" href="">&larr; Portfolio</a>

    <section class="hero">
        <p class="eyebrow">The @P.Archetype</p>
        <h1 class="title">@P.Name</h1>
        <p class="tagline">@P.Tagline</p>
        <p class="status">
            @P.Status
            @if (P.GitHubUrl is not null)
            {
                <span class="dot">·</span>
                <a href="@P.GitHubUrl" target="_blank" rel="noopener">GitHub</a>
            }
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="prose">
        <h2>The problem</h2>
        <p>
            Real-time conferencing is rarely just a video call. There is a calendar on
            one side and a media backend on the other, and most platforms hard-wire you
            to a single choice for each. Swap a vendor and the center has to learn a
            new dialect.
        </p>

        <h2>The approach</h2>
        <p>
            RoomLoom treats scheduling and media as ports the core owns. Calendars and
            video systems plug in as adapters behind those contracts. The core never
            knows which vendor it is talking to. Adding a new system means writing one
            adapter, not editing the center — a single thread on a loom whose frame
            stays still.
        </p>

        <h2>What it shows</h2>
        <p>
            A hexagonal architecture in production .NET, with SignalR carrying real-time
            state, EF Core persisting it, MAUI shipping the same primitives to mobile,
            and Azure + GitHub Actions doing the rest.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section class="stack">
        <p class="stack-label">Stack</p>
        <div class="chips">
            @foreach (var tech in P.Stack)
            {
                <span class="chip">@tech</span>
            }
        </div>
    </section>
</div>

@code {
    private static readonly Project P = ProjectsCatalog.All.First(p => p.Slug == "roomloom");
}
